| 4    | –0.01, 0.02, –0.01, 0.07 | –0.8·θ̇ = –0.056 (Rule 2) | `u_raw ≈ -12·0.02 -6·0.07 -1·(–0.01) -2·(–0.01) ≈ -0.24 -0.42 +0.01 +0.02 = -0.63` → stronger left push, preventing the angle from growing. |
| 9    | –0.03, 0.07, –0.33, 0.86 | –0.8·θ̇ = –0.688 (Rule 2) | `u_raw ≈ -12·0.07 -6·0.86 -1·(–0.03) -2·(–0.33) ≈ -0.84 -5.16 +0.03 +0.66 = -5.31` → clipped to –3 N (max left). The new controller would also saturate, but it would have already applied a larger corrective torque earlier, so the pole would not have reached such a large angle. |

The comparison shows that **Strategy C often waited** (zero‑force or very small force) while the pole angle was already drifting, whereas the LQR‑style policy pushes continuously and more aggressively.

---

### Bottom line

*The most effective strategy so far is the hand‑crafted rule set (average ≈ 17 s), but it still suffers from overly large “do‑nothing” zones and modest gains.*  
**Adopting a simple linear state‑feedback controller with the gains shown above** should roughly double the average episode length while keeping the implementation straightforward. Feel free to fine‑tune the four gains (e.g. increase `K_theta` to –14 or reduce `K_x` if the cart drifts too much) – the structure of the policy will remain the same.
