| “A linear combination of all four states works everywhere.” | **Partially right** – it works *once* the pole has deviated, but it needs to be *scaled* according to the current angle. | The full‑state feedback is essential for recovery, but the scaling must be angle‑dependent. |
| “Clipping the force after a tanh‑soft‑limit is sufficient to avoid instability.” | **Right** – the tanh keeps the command smooth and bounded. | No spikes are observed when the tanh is used. |

### 3️⃣ Revised control policy (the one we will adopt)

Below is a **compact pseudo‑code** that combines the lessons above.  
It keeps the pole upright for many more steps (the empirical average of the previous best run was already ~25 % of the episode length; with the tweaks below we expect > 35 % on average).

```text
IF NOT isfinite(x) OR NOT isfinite(x_dot) OR NOT isfinite(theta) OR NOT isfinite(theta_dot)
THEN
    force = 0.0                                 # safety net
ELSE
    # -------------------------------------------------
    # 1.  Very small pole deviation → pure cart PD
    # -------------------------------------------------
    IF abs(theta) < 0.015                         # tighter than 0.02
    THEN
        u = -(k_pos * x + k_vel * x_dot)          # k_pos≈0.8, k_vel≈0.4
    ELSE
        # -------------------------------------------------
        # 2.  Moderate / large deviation → full‑state feedback
        # -------------------------------------------------
        # Base linear controller (tuned LQR‑like gains)
        u_base = -(k1 * x + k2 * x_dot + k3 * theta + k4 * theta_dot)

        # Angle‑dependent gain scaling:
        #   gain = 1   at |θ| = 0.015
        #   gain = 3   at |θ| = 0.20
        #   linear interpolation in‑between
        gain = 1.0 + 2.0 * (abs(theta) - 0.015) / (0.20 - 0.015)
        IF gain > 3.0 THEN gain = 3.0

        u = gain * u_base
    ENDIF

    # -------------------------------------------------
    # 3.  Soft saturation (keeps the command smooth)
    # -------------------------------------------------
    u_soft = 3.0 * tanh(u / 3.0)          # maps any u → [-3, +3]

    # -------------------------------------------------
    # 4.  Final clipping (just in case)
    # -------------------------------------------------
    IF u_soft > 3.0   THEN force = 3.0
    ELIF u_soft < -3.0 THEN force = -3.0
    ELSE               force = u_soft
    ENDIF
ENDIF
```

