**2. New “balanced‑pole” policy**

Below is a compact, self‑contained rule set that incorporates the lessons above.  
All arithmetic is done in floating‑point; `clamp(v, lo, hi)` returns `max(lo, min(v, hi))`.

```text
# --------------------------------------------------------------
#  Pole‑Balancing Controller (continuous force ∈ [‑3, 3] N)
# --------------------------------------------------------------
# State variables:
#   x      – cart position (m)
#   theta  – pole angle (rad)   (0 = upright)
#   x_dot  – cart velocity (m/s)
#   th_dot – pole angular velocity (rad/s)

# Safety check
IF any of (x, theta, x_dot, th_dot) is not finite THEN
    force ← 0.0
    RETURN force
END IF

# Failure check – if we are already out of bounds we do nothing
IF |theta| ≥ 0.20 THEN
    force ← 0.0
    RETURN force
END IF

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Gains (tuned by hand, close to an LQR solution)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
K_theta   ← 12.0      # angle proportional gain
K_thdot   ← 5.0       # angle derivative gain
K_x       ← 0.8       # cart‑position proportional gain
K_xdot    ← 0.4       # cart‑velocity derivative gain

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Determine which “mode” we are in
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
theta_abs ← |theta|
theta_small ← 0.02          # inner dead‑band (rad)
theta_mid   ← 0.08          # start of stronger scaling (rad)
theta_max   ← 0.20          # failure limit (rad)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Base control laws (no scaling yet)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
u_angle ← -(K_theta * theta + K_thdot * th_dot)      # tries to bring pole upright
u_cart  ← -(K_x * x + K_xdot * x_dot)               # tries to keep cart near centre

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Blend cart‑bias depending on how far we are from vertical
#    – full cart bias when |θ| ≤ theta_small
#    – linearly fade to zero at theta_mid
#    – zero beyond theta_mid
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
IF theta_abs ≤ theta_small THEN
    cart_factor ← 1.0
ELSE IF theta_abs < theta_mid THEN
    cart_factor ← 1.0 - (theta_abs - theta_small) / (theta_mid - theta_small)
ELSE
    cart_factor ← 0.0
END IF

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Scaling of the total command
#    – No scaling (factor = 1) inside the tiny‑angle band
#    – Linear increase to a maximum of 2.0 at the failure bound
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
scale ← 1.0
IF theta_abs > theta_small THEN
    scale ← 1.0 + (theta_abs - theta_small) * (2.0 - 1.0) / (theta_max - theta_small)
    # clamp just in case of numerical drift
    IF scale > 2.0 THEN scale ← 2.0
END IF

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Assemble the raw command
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
raw_force ← scale * (u_angle + cart_factor * u_cart)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Saturate to actuator limits
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
force ← clamp(raw_force, -3.0, 3.0)

RETURN force
```

**Why this should work better**

| Component | Reason |
|-----------|--------|
| **Small‑angle dead‑band (θ < 0.02 rad)** | Keeps the cart‑bias fully active when the pole is almost upright, preventing the cart from drifting away. |
| **Linear fade‑out of cart bias (θ ∈ [0.02, 0.08])** | As the pole tilts, the cart‑bias starts to fight the corrective torque; fading it out removes that antagonism while still providing some centre‑keeping early on. |
| **Gentle scaling (max × 2)** | Gives the controller a little extra “muscle” as the pole leans, but never pushes the actuator into hard saturation, which was the main cause of the –3 N spikes in the earlier runs. |
| **Separate proportional/derivative gains for angle and cart** | Mirrors a classic LQR design; the chosen numbers (12/5 for angle, 0.8/0.4 for cart) are the same values that produced the highest scores in the earlier trials. |
| **Safety & failure checks** | Guarantees we never issue a command when the episode is already lost or the state is corrupted. |

