## 3. Why this should beat the previous strategy  

| Aspect | Old strategy | New strategy |
|--------|--------------|--------------|
| **Dead‑zone width** | |θ| ≤ 0.02 rad (ignores angular terms) | |θ| ≤ 0.01 rad (still tiny, but angular terms appear earlier) |
| **Angular gain** | 10.0 | 25.0 (≈ 2.5× stronger) |
| **Angular‑velocity gain** | 2.5 | 5.0 (≈ 2× stronger) |
| **Handling of moderate tilt** | Same linear law as “large‑θ” branch → weak until |θ| ≥ 0.12 rad | Separate “moderate” branch with full linear law, giving a smoother increase of torque |
| **Safety fallback** | `force = -3 * sign(theta)` only when |θ| ≥ 0.12 rad | Same fallback, but it is reached later (after the stronger linear law has already slowed the pole) |
| **Numerical robustness** | No explicit casting → booleans could leak into arithmetic | All inputs cast to `float` → eliminates the `numpy boolean subtract` error |

With the stronger angular feedback the pole is pulled back to the vertical much faster, preventing the exponential growth of θ̇ that caused the crash in the last trial. The narrower dead‑zone also avoids the “drift while we pretend the pole is perfectly upright” problem.

