| `[0.00, 0.05, -0.01, 0.21]` (|θ| ≈ 0.01) | –0.0 | cart_pd ≈ ‑0.025 → raw ≈ ‑0.025 | ≈ ‑0.025 |
| `[0.00, 0.09, -0.02, 0.40]` (|θ| ≈ 0.02) | –0.0 | cart_pd ≈ ‑0.045 → raw ≈ ‑0.045 | ≈ ‑0.045 |
| `[‑0.01, 0.19, -0.03, 0.79]` (|θ| ≈ 0.03) | –3.0 (hard‑stop) | α≈0.06 → kθ≈8.96, kθ̇≈2.24 → pole_pd≈‑0.27, cart_pd≈‑0.095 → raw≈‑0.365 | ≈ ‑0.35 (no need for full –3 N yet) |
| `[‑0.03, 0.27, -1.03, 3.15]` (|θ| ≈ 1.03) | None (episode ended) | α≈1 → kθ=20, kθ̇=6 → pole_pd≈‑(20·‑1.03 + 6·3.15) ≈ ‑(‑20.6 + 18.9) ≈ ‑‑1.7 ≈ 1.7; cart_pd≈‑0.165 → raw≈ 1.535 → force≈ tanh(1.535)·3 ≈ 2.6 N (still within limits, but the pole is already far beyond the safe region, so the episode would end anyway). |

The controller therefore **delays the use of full‑strength force** until it is truly needed, while still providing enough corrective torque earlier to keep the pole from reaching the failure angle. In the medium‑angle regime the force stays well below the actuator limit, which reduces the “jerk” that previously caused the pole to swing back and forth.

