| **|θ| ≤ 0.02 rad** | Only cart‑position terms (`‑0.8 x – 0.4 ẋ`). | The pole is already almost upright; we avoid injecting unnecessary pole‑torque that would cause jitter. |
| **0.02 rad < |θ| ≤ 0.12 rad** | Full linear state‑feedback (`x, ẋ, θ, θ̇`). | The pole is still relatively close, so a proportional‑derivative term on the pole together with a modest cart‑stabiliser can gently bring it back without large overshoots. |
| **|θ| > 0.12 rad** | Stronger pole‑only term (`‑12 θ – 6 θ̇`) plus a tiny cart bias. | The pole is falling fast; we need a larger corrective torque, but we keep it *graded* (depends on both angle and angular velocity) instead of a hard bang‑bang. The extra `‑0.3 x` term prevents the cart from drifting to the edge while the emergency push is applied. |

All gains are **order‑of‑magnitude** consistent with the physics of the classic OpenAI‑Gym CartPole (mass ≈ 1 kg, pole length ≈ 0.5 m).  They were obtained by a quick manual sweep and give a **stable “soft‑landing”** for most initial disturbances.

