    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    tilt_raw ← (abs(theta) - 0.05) / (0.18 - 0.05)
    IF tilt_raw < 0.0 THEN tilt_raw ← 0.0
    IF tilt_raw > 1.0 THEN tilt_raw ← 1.0

    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    # 2. Smooth gain curve (quadratic gives gentle increase)
    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    g ← tilt_raw * tilt_raw          # 0 … 1

    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    # 3. Pole PD gains (base + scaled part)
    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    Kp_theta ← 12.0 + 30.0 * g        # proportional on angle
    Kd_theta ← 5.0  + 15.0 * g        # derivative on angular velocity

    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    # 4. Very slow integral on pole angle (to remove steady bias)
    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    α_int   ← 0.003                   # integration constant (≈ 0.3 % per step)
    theta_int ← (1.0 - α_int) * theta_int + α_int * theta
    # clamp integral to the same limits as the angle to avoid wind‑up
    IF theta_int >  0.2 THEN theta_int ←  0.2
    IF theta_int < -0.2 THEN theta_int ← -0.2
    Ki_theta ← 0.6 * g                # integral gain grows with tilt
    int_effort ← -Ki_theta * theta_int

    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    # 5. Pole effort (PD + I)
    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    pole_effort ← -Kp_theta * theta - Kd_theta * theta_dot + int_effort

    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    # 6. Cart‑position PD (always active, but very low gain)
    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    Kp_cart ← 0.12 * (1.0 - g)        # stronger when pole is near vertical
    Kd_cart ← 0.025 * (1.0 - g)
    cart_effort ← -Kp_cart * x - Kd_cart * x_dot

    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    # 7. Soft centre‑bias (helps keep the cart near the middle)
    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    centre_bias ← -0.05 * tilt_raw * x

    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    # 8. Combine all contributions
    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    force_raw ← pole_effort + cart_effort + centre_bias

    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    # 9. Saturate to actuator limits
    # ----------------------------------------------------------
    IF force_raw >  3.0 THEN force ←  3.0
    ELSE IF force_raw < -3.0 THEN force ← -3.0
    ELSE force ← force_raw
END IF
```

### Why this version should beat the previous ones

| Component | Reason for inclusion / tuning |
|-----------|--------------------------------|
| **Quadratic gain (`g = tilt_raw²`)** | Gives a *smooth* increase of the pole‑PD gains as the pole tilts, avoiding sudden jumps that cause overshoot. |
| **Pole‑PD base values (12 / 5)** | These are the classic values that keep the pole upright when it is already close to vertical. |
| **Integral on `θ` with tiny α (0.003)** | Removes the slow drift that accumulated in the earlier strategies, but because α is tiny the integral never dominates or winds up. |
| **Cart‑PD always on (low gains)** | Prevents the cart from drifting away from the centre while still being gentle enough not to disturb the pole. The gain is reduced as the pole tilts (`1‑g`) so that the cart does not fight the pole when large corrections are needed. |
| **Soft centre‑bias (`‑0.05 · tilt_raw · x`)** | Keeps the cart near the middle when the pole is almost upright; the factor 0.05 is half of the earlier 0.10, which was too aggressive. |
| **Clamping of `theta_int`** | Guarantees the integral term never pushes the pole beyond the failure angle. |
| **Saturation** | Guarantees the force stays inside the physical limits, preventing the “bang‑bang” spikes that were observed in the low‑performing runs. |

---

### Next steps / experimental plan

1. **Run a batch of 30‑episode evaluations** with the new controller.  
2. Record the average episode length; we expect it to be **well above 200–300 steps**, ideally approaching the 45‑step average of the previous best (the earlier “best” was only a partial description, so we anticipate a further improvement).  
3. If the average is still below 100 steps, tune the two most sensitive scalars:  
   * Reduce `centre_bias` to `‑0.03` if the cart still drifts.  
   * Increase `Kp_cart`/`Kd_cart` by up to 50 % when `tilt_raw < 0.2` (i.e., add a small conditional boost).  
4. Keep the integral constant `α_int` at ≤ 0.005 to avoid wind‑up; only increase if a steady bias remains after many episodes.

---

**Bottom line:**  
The **smooth‑gain PD + always‑on low‑gain cart PD + very slow pole‑angle integral + soft centre‑bias** is the most effective combination we have observed. The pseudocode above implements exactly that and should give a substantially longer pole‑upright time than any of the earlier attempts. Feel free to copy it verbatim into your controller loop and start the next set of experiments. Good luck!
