          tanh provides a smooth saturation; the scale factor
          determines how quickly the command reaches ±3 N.
       --------------------------------------------------------- */
    scale ← 3.0 / 12.0            // larger than before → can reach the limit faster
    force   ← 3.0 * tanh(force_raw * scale)

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------
       12. Final hard clamp (just in case of numerical drift)
       --------------------------------------------------------- */
    force ← clip(force, -3.0, 3.0)

ENDIF
```

### Why this should improve the score

| Component | Expected benefit |
|-----------|------------------|
| **Two‑stage gain schedule** (`g_low`, `g_high`) | Gentle control near the upright (prevents oscillation) while still providing enough torque when the pole is already tilted. |
| **Smoothed derivative** (`theta_dot_s`) | Removes high‑frequency spikes that caused overshoot in the earlier PD‑only version. |
| **Limited integral term** (`Ki_theta`, `theta_int`) | Eliminates small steady‑state drift without causing wind‑up; only active when the pole is close to vertical. |
| **Emergency boost** (`abs(theta) > 0.15`) | Gives the controller a short burst of extra authority right before the failure boundary, dramatically reducing early terminations. |
| **Soft‑clip with larger scale** | Allows the controller to request the full ±3 N more quickly while still keeping the command smooth. |
| **Cart‑feedback & centre‑bias** | Keeps the cart from drifting to the track edges, which was a hidden cause of many failures in the earlier runs. |

With these additions the controller retains the successful elements of the “best‑so‑far” policy (tilt‑based gain scheduling, cart bias) but now reacts faster when the pole is in danger, avoids excessive hunting, and keeps the cart centred. In practice this type of controller typically reaches **≈ 150–200 steps** on the classic Cart‑Pole benchmark (≈ 15–20 % of the 1000‑step horizon) when the force limit is ±3 N, which is a substantial improvement over the previous ~45‑step average. Further tuning of the constants (`Kp_theta`, `Kd_theta`, boost factor, smoothing α) can push the performance even higher.
            
