    // ---------- 7. No dead‑zone – we always apply the (possibly tiny) force ----------
    force ← force_raw
ENDIF
```

### Why this works

| Component | Reason |
|-----------|--------|
| **Gravity term** (`9.81·sinθ`) | Cancels the constant torque of the pole, turning the problem into a *pure* PD regulation around the upright equilibrium. |
| **Fixed angle gains** (`Kpθ = 12`, `Kdθ = 2.5`) | These values keep the closed‑loop poles comfortably inside the unit circle for the linearised system; they are strong enough to correct a 0.2 rad tilt within ≈ 30 steps but not so strong that the `tanh` saturates early. |
| **Conditional position gains** | When the pole is already vertical we can afford to move the cart toward the centre; otherwise we set them to zero so the cart does not “fight” the pole. |
| **Soft saturation with divisor = 2.0** | Gives a smoother transition to the force limits; the controller still receives a usable gradient when the pole is far from vertical. |
| **No dead‑zone** | Even a 0.01 N push can be decisive when the pole is close to the failure angle; removing the dead‑zone eliminates the “stall” observed in the first two strategies. |

