# --------------------------------------------------------------
# 5️⃣ Smooth saturation (tanh) and hard clipping
# --------------------------------------------------------------
#   Scale the tanh so that a raw value of ±1.0 already produces the
#   maximum admissible force (±3 N).  The divisor “1.0” can be
#   tweaked – a larger divisor makes the controller less aggressive.
force ← 3.0 * tanh( u_raw / 1.0 )

#   (tanh already limits the output to (‑3, +3), but we keep an
#    explicit clip for numerical safety.)
IF force > 3.0  THEN force ← 3.0 END IF
IF force < -3.0 THEN force ← -3.0 END IF

RETURN force
```

#### Why this works better  

| Component | Reason it improves the reward |
|-----------|------------------------------|
| **Tilt‑norm** (0 → 1) | Gives the controller a *graded* response – gentle when the pole is almost upright, strong when it is close to the ±0.2 rad limit. |
| **Interpolated gains** | The soft gains keep the cart from “wiggling” around the centre; the hard gains give enough torque to pull the pole back before it crosses the failure angle. |
| **Both pole‑ and cart‑terms at all times** | Prevents the cart from drifting to the edges, which otherwise forces a large corrective force that would saturate. |
| **Gravity feed‑forward (`g·sinθ`)** | Cancels the dominant static torque of the pole, so the PD part only has to deal with the *dynamic* error. |
| **`tanh` squashing** | Provides a smooth transition into saturation, avoiding the “bang‑bang” behaviour that destabilises the system. |
| **Explicit clipping** | Guarantees the action stays inside the allowed range even if numerical errors push `tanh` slightly outside `[-1, 1]`. |

