## 2. New/Updated Strategy

Below is a complete, self‑contained rule set that incorporates all the lessons above.  
It is written in a pseudo‑code style that matches the previous examples, but you can translate it directly into any programming language.

```text
/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   CART‑POLE BALANCING CONTROLLER
   -------------------------------------------------------------
   State vector:  [x, x_dot, theta, theta_dot]
   Action:        force ∈ [‑3.0 , 3.0]   (N)
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */

IF NOT ( isfinite(x) AND isfinite(x_dot) AND
         isfinite(theta) AND isfinite(theta_dot) ) THEN
    force ← 0.0
ELSE
    /* ---------------------------------------------------------
       1️⃣  Normalise the tilt – we need a value in [0,1] that
           tells us how close we are to the failure angle.
           The “safe” region is |θ| ≤ 0.05 rad, the “critical”
           region is |θ| ≥ 0.18 rad.
       --------------------------------------------------------- */
    tilt_abs      ← abs(theta)
    tilt_norm_raw ← (tilt_abs - 0.05) / (0.18 - 0.05)   // 0 → safe, 1 → critical
    IF tilt_norm_raw < 0.0 THEN
        tilt_norm ← 0.0
    ELSE IF tilt_norm_raw > 1.0 THEN
        tilt_norm ← 1.0
    ELSE
        tilt_norm ← tilt_norm_raw
    END IF

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------
       2️⃣  Gain scheduling – linearly interpolate between a
           “gentle” gain set (when the pole is near vertical) and
           an “aggressive” gain set (when the pole is close to the
           limits).  The interpolation factor is tilt_norm.
       --------------------------------------------------------- */
    // Base gains (safe region)
    Kp_theta_safe ← 12.0      // proportional on angle
    Kd_theta_safe ←  8.0      // derivative on angle
    Kp_x_safe     ←  0.5
    Kd_x_safe     ←  0.5

    // Aggressive gains (critical region)
    Kp_theta_aggr ← 30.0
    Kd_theta_aggr ← 20.0
    Kp_x_aggr     ← 1.5
    Kd_x_aggr     ← 1.2

    // Interpolated gains
    Kp_theta ← (1.0 - tilt_norm) * Kp_theta_safe + tilt_norm * Kp_theta_aggr
    Kd_theta ← (1.0 - tilt_norm) * Kd_theta_safe + tilt_norm * Kd_theta_aggr
    Kp_x     ← (1.0 - tilt_norm) * Kp_x_safe     + tilt_norm * Kp_x_aggr
    Kd_x     ← (1.0 - tilt_norm) * Kd_x_safe     + tilt_norm * Kd_x_aggr

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------
       3️⃣  Emergency boost – when the pole is *very* close to the
           failure angle we add a short‑burst bias that pushes the
           cart in the direction of the tilt.  The boost grows from
           0 at |θ| = 0.18 to a maximum of 8 N at |θ| = 0.20.
       --------------------------------------------------------- */
    IF tilt_abs > 0.18 THEN
        boost_factor ← (tilt_abs - 0.18) / (0.20 - 0.18)   // 0 → 1
        boost       ← 8.0 * boost_factor * sign(theta)   // N, before clipping
    ELSE
        boost ← 0.0
    END IF

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------
       4️⃣  Raw control law (PD on angle + PD on cart + boost)
       --------------------------------------------------------- */
    u_raw ← -( Kp_theta * theta
               + Kd_theta * theta_dot
               + Kp_x     * x
               + Kd_x     * x_dot ) + boost

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------
       5️⃣  Gravity compensation – add the torque that gravity
           would exert on the pole.  This term is small (≈ 0‑9 N)
           but helps the controller near the vertical position.
       --------------------------------------------------------- */
    u_raw ← u_raw + 9.81 * sin(theta)

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------
       6️⃣  Map the unlimited control signal to the admissible
           force range.  We use a tanh with a divisor that lets
           the controller reach the ±3 N limits when |u_raw| is
           roughly 6 N (i.e. divisor = 2.0).  Afterwards we clip
           explicitly to be safe against numerical overshoot.
       --------------------------------------------------------- */
    force_unclipped ← 3.0 * tanh( u_raw / 2.0 )   // range ≈ [‑3, 3]

    IF force_unclipped > 3.0 THEN
        force ← 3.0
    ELSE IF force_unclipped < -3.0 THEN
        force ← -3.0
    ELSE
        force ← force_unclipped
    END IF
END IF
```

### Why this should work better

| Component | Reason |
|-----------|--------|
| **Tilt‑norm & gain‑scheduling** | Gives a *soft* transition from gentle balancing (low gains → smooth motion) to aggressive catching (high gains) exactly when the pole is close to falling. |
| **Higher base gains** (`Kp_theta≈12‑30`, `Kd_theta≈8‑20`) | The earlier controllers used gains that were too low to generate enough torque; the new values are in the range that classic cart‑pole PD‑controllers use. |
| **Explicit emergency boost** | When |θ| > 0.18 rad the controller can instantly request the maximum available force in the correct direction, preventing the pole from crossing the failure angle. |
| **Gravity term added *after* the PD** | Keeps the PD law clean; the sin θ term simply offsets the constant gravity torque without interfering with the gain tuning. |
| **tanh divisor = 2.0** | Allows the controller to reach the ±3 N limit when the PD output is about ±6 N, which is roughly the torque needed at the edge of the safe region. |
| **Clipping after tanh** | Guarantees the final command respects the actuator limits even if numerical errors occur. |

