| Component | Reason it improves the score |
|-----------|------------------------------|
| **Early tilt normalisation (0.02 rad → 0.20 rad)** | Gives the controller a *graded* sense of urgency instead of a binary “safe / unsafe”. |
| **Linear gain‑scheduling** | When the pole is almost upright we use modest gains → smooth, low‑amplitude corrections; when it leans, the gains jump → strong, fast recovery. |
| **Boost that starts at 0.12 rad** | The pole never reaches the “danger” region (≈ 0.18 rad) without already receiving extra torque, so the failure angle is rarely hit. |
| **Gravity term ( + g·sinθ )** | Cancels the constant torque created by the pole’s weight; the PD controller only has to deal with the *dynamic* part of the error. |
| **tanh‑based saturation** | Prevents harsh clipping that would introduce discontinuities; the controller sees a smooth relationship between error and output up to the physical limit of ±3 N. |
| **Safety check** | Guarantees that a NaN/Inf never propagates into the control law, avoiding immediate termination. |

