| **Safety check** | Guarantees we never send a NaN/Inf to the plant. |
| **Tilt normalisation (0.05 rad dead‑zone)** | Gives the controller a “comfort zone” where it can be very gentle; the pole never receives a large corrective impulse while it is already stable. |
| **Linear gain schedule** | Gains increase smoothly as the pole leans more, providing stronger correction *only when needed*. The chosen ranges (Kpθ ≈ 8‑25, Kdθ ≈ 4‑12) are well‑inside the region that keeps the command below the ±3 N limit for most of the trajectory. |
| **Separate gravity term** | Adding `9.81·sin(theta)` after the PD term means the PD part does not have to “fight” gravity; it only has to correct the *error* around the equilibrium. |
| **Modest boost** | A small extra push (≤ ±1.5 N) helps when the pole is already close to the failure angle, but because it is limited it rarely forces the command into saturation. |
| **tanh‑based saturation** | Provides a smooth transition to the actuator limits, avoiding the harsh discontinuity of a hard clamp and keeping the controller’s Jacobian well‑behaved for the next step. |
| **Clamping at the very end** | Guarantees we never exceed the physical limits even if the tanh approximation drifts because of extreme `u_raw`. |

