**2. Which assumptions were right / wrong?**

| Assumption | Verdict |
|------------|---------|
| “Higher proportional gain = better stability” | **Partly true** – but only when the gain is *scaled* with the amount of tilt.  A constant high gain makes the controller too aggressive near the centre and leads to oscillations that push the pole out of the safe band. |
| “An integral term will cancel steady‑state error” | **Wrong** for this problem.  The pole dynamics are fast and the error never settles; the integrator just accumulates a large bias that drives the cart into the limits. |
| “A hard dead‑zone prevents jitter” | **Wrong** – the dead‑zone removes the tiny corrective forces that are needed when the pole is almost upright, so the pole drifts until the tilt exceeds the dead‑zone and then a large corrective burst is applied, which overshoots. |
| “A very steep sigmoid on the tilt will give a crisp switch between “low‑gain” and “high‑gain” regimes” | **Wrong** – a steep switch creates a discontinuity in the control law that the plant cannot follow smoothly.  The best trace shows a *smooth* sigmoid (≈ 12 × (tau‑0.5) in the exponent) which gives a gradual transition. |
| “Clipping the raw command with tanh is enough” | **True** – the tanh‑based saturation is smooth and respects the ±3 N limit without creating a hard cut‑off. |

