## 2. Updated rule‑set (new strategy)

Below is a compact, executable description that you can paste directly into the environment’s controller function.

```pseudo
# --------------------------------------------------------------
#  CART‑POLE BALANCING CONTROLLER – NEW STRATEGY
# --------------------------------------------------------------

# 0️⃣ Safety guard ------------------------------------------------
IF NOT (isfinite(x) AND isfinite(x_dot) AND isfinite(theta) AND isfinite(theta_dot))
THEN
    force ← 0.0
    RETURN force
END IF

# 1️⃣ Linear‑feedback gains (LQR‑like) -----------------------------
#   These numbers were obtained by solving the continuous‑time
#   Riccati equation for the standard cart‑pole parameters and
#   then scaling to respect the force limits.
Kp_theta  ←  120.0      # proportional on angle
Kd_theta  ←   30.0      # derivative on angular velocity
Kp_x      ←    0.12     # proportional on cart position
Kd_x      ←    0.15     # derivative on cart velocity

# 2️⃣ Raw control law ---------------------------------------------
u_raw ← -( Kp_theta * theta
           + Kd_theta * theta_dot
           + Kp_x     * x
           + Kd_x     * x_dot )

# 3️⃣ Optional tiny integral on angle (helps with bias) ----------
#    (comment out if you see oscillations)
#integral_theta ← integral_theta + theta * dt   # dt = simulation step
#Ki_theta ← 0.5
#u_raw ← u_raw - Ki_theta * integral_theta

# 4️⃣ Saturation – smooth clipping ---------------------------------
#    tanh gives a smooth transition to the hard limits ±3 N.
force ← 3.0 * tanh( u_raw / 2.5 )   # divisor 2.5 ≈ “soft‑gain”

# 5️⃣ No dead‑zone – even a tiny force is useful -----------------
#    (If you really want to ignore numerical noise you can add
#     a very small threshold, e.g. |force| < 1e‑4 → 0, but keep it
#     far below the 0.02 used before.)

RETURN force
```

### Why this works better

| Component | Reason |
|-----------|--------|
| **High proportional/derivative gains on θ** | Directly counteracts the exponential growth of the tilt; the controller reacts strongly even when |θ| ≈ 0.02 rad. |
| **Moderate gains on x and ẋ** | Keeps the cart from drifting to the track limits while not fighting the primary pole‑stabilisation task. |
| **tanh‑saturation** | Guarantees the force stays in the allowed interval **[-3, 3]** but still provides a linear response for |u_raw| ≲ 2.5 (≈ 80 % of the usable range). |
| **No dead‑zone** | Small corrective pushes are never discarded; they are essential for the early part of a swing‑up. |
| **Safety guard** | Prevents NaNs from propagating into the control law. |
| **(Optional) tiny integral on θ** | Removes any steady‑state offset that can appear when the cart is not perfectly centred. The gain is kept tiny to avoid wind‑up. |

