## 2️⃣ Updated control rule‑set (pseudo‑code)

```pseudo
# --------------------------------------------------------------
#  Pole‑Balancing Controller – “Fast‑PD‑Schedule”
# --------------------------------------------------------------
# Safety check
IF NOT finite(x) OR NOT finite(x_dot) OR NOT finite(theta) OR NOT finite(theta_dot) THEN
    force ← 0.0
ELSE
    # ---- 1. Normalised tilt (linear schedule) -----------------
    #   dead‑zone up to 0.05 rad, full gain at 0.18 rad
    tilt_raw ← (abs(theta) - 0.05) / (0.18 - 0.05)
    IF tilt_raw < 0.0 THEN tilt_raw ← 0.0
    IF tilt_raw > 1.0 THEN tilt_raw ← 1.0
    #   use the raw value (linear) – no squaring/cubing
    tilt ← tilt_raw

    # ---- 2. Gain scheduling -----------------------------------
    #   Base gains (when pole is almost vertical)
    Kp_base ← 30.0      # pole‑angle proportional
    Kd_base ← 2.0       # pole‑angle derivative
    #   Additional gain when tilt grows
    Kp_gain ← 150.0 * tilt   # final Kp ≈ 180 at max tilt
    Kd_gain ← 10.0  * tilt   # final Kd ≈ 12  at max tilt

    Kp ← Kp_base + Kp_gain
    Kd ← Kd_base + Kd_gain

    # ---- 3. Cart‑centering PD (small) -------------------------
    Kx  ← 0.5   * tilt   # grows with tilt, keeps cart near 0
    Kv  ← 0.1   * tilt

    # ---- 4. Gravity feed‑forward ------------------------------
    ff_grav ← 0.15 * sin(theta)      # simple compensation

    # ---- 5. Raw control law -----------------------------------
    u_raw ← -(Kp * theta + Kd * theta_dot + ff_grav) \
             - (Kx * x + Kv * x_dot)

    # ---- 6. Hard saturation (±3 N) ----------------------------
    IF u_raw > 3.0 THEN
        force ← 3.0
    ELSE IF u_raw < -3.0 THEN
        force ← -3.0
    ELSE
        force ← u_raw
    ENDIF
ENDIF
```

### Why this should work better

| Component | Expected effect |
|-----------|-----------------|
| **Linear tilt‑schedule** | Gains start increasing as soon as the pole leaves the dead‑zone (≈ 0.05 rad). The controller therefore has enough torque before the pole reaches the failure angle (0.2 rad). |
| **Higher Kp/Kd at large tilt** | Provides the strong corrective torque needed when the pole is far from vertical, without overshooting when it is near upright. |
| **Small cart‑centering PD** | Prevents the cart from drifting to the track edges, which would otherwise limit the usable force budget. The term is scaled with `tilt` so it does not interfere when the pole is already stable. |
| **Light gravity feed‑forward** | Cancels the static component of the pole’s weight, reducing the burden on the PD loop. |
| **No integral term** | Avoids wind‑up and keeps the response fast. |
| **Hard clipping** | Guarantees the maximum admissible force is always available when the controller demands it. |

---

## 3️⃣ How to evaluate the new rule‑set

1. **Run 20 fresh trials** (each up to 1000 steps).  
